"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkres_id_au||=[]).push([[48130],{77735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/09/18/lambda-web-visitor-counter","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026/09/18/lambda-web-visitor-counter","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-18-lambda-web-visitor-counter.mdx","title":"Walkthrough: Web Visitor Counter Lambda","description":"Inspired by Cloud Resume Challenge, I started building a high-performance, strongly typed AWS Lambda function backed by Amazon DynamoDB for atomic web visitor counting, triggered via Amazon API Gateway HTTP API (Payload v2).","date":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Walkthrough","permalink":"/blog/tags/walkthrough","description":"Step-by-step walkthroughs and tutorials"},{"inline":false,"label":"Lambda","permalink":"/blog/tags/lambda","description":"AWS Lambda and serverless content"},{"inline":false,"label":"DynamoDB","permalink":"/blog/tags/dynamodb","description":"AWS DynamoDB content"},{"inline":false,"label":"API Gateway","permalink":"/blog/tags/api-gateway","description":"AWS API Gateway content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Node.js","permalink":"/blog/tags/nodejs","description":"Node.js content"},{"inline":false,"label":"TypeScript","permalink":"/blog/tags/typescript","description":"TypeScript specific content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Visitor Counter API","permalink":"/blog/tags/visitor-counter-api","description":"Visitor Counter API content"}],"readingTime":2.74,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Rendy E. Saputra","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/rendy"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra/","github":"https://github.com/rensaputra","Website":"https://res.id.au"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/rensaputra.png","key":"rendy"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Walkthrough: Web Visitor Counter Lambda","authors":["rendy"],"tags":["walkthrough","lambda","dynamodb","api-gateway","nodejs","typescript","visitor-counter-api"],"date":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"How to setup Husky and Lint-Staged","permalink":"/blog/2026/09/17/how-to-setup-1-husky-and-lint-staged"}},"content":"Inspired by [Cloud Resume Challenge](https://cloudresumechallenge.dev/docs/the-challenge/aws/#7-javascript), I started building a high-performance, strongly typed AWS Lambda function backed by Amazon DynamoDB for atomic web visitor counting, triggered via Amazon API Gateway HTTP API (Payload v2).\\n\\nCurrently I have finished working on the Lambda function, and I am working towards the deployment of the API Gateway HTTP API and DynamoDB table. This post is a walkthrough of the source code, testing, and build process, that I and AI pair-programmed together. The source code is available on [GitHub](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What Was Built\\n\\n### 1. Source Architecture\\n\\n- [`src/types.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/src/types.ts): Strongly typed models for `CounterRecord`, `CounterResponse`, `ErrorResponse`, and `AppConfig`.\\n- [`src/config.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/src/config.ts): Environment variable loader for `TABLE_NAME`, `ALLOW_ORIGIN`, `DEFAULT_PAGE_ID`, and `AWS_REGION`.\\n- [`src/utils.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/src/utils.ts): CORS headers, API Gateway v2 response builders, and `pageId` sanitization (validates characters and restricts max length to 128 characters).\\n- [`src/counter.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/src/counter.ts): DynamoDB atomic operations using AWS SDK v3 `DynamoDBDocumentClient`:\\n  - `incrementCounter`: Uses atomic update expression:\\n    ```typescript\\n    SET #count = if_not_exists(#count, :zero) + :inc, #updatedAt = :now\\n    ```\\n  - `getCounter`: Reads count without incrementing; returns `{ count: 0, updatedAt: null }` for unvisited pages.\\n- [`src/handler.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/src/handler.ts): Main AWS Lambda handler (`APIGatewayProxyHandlerV2`):\\n  - Handles `OPTIONS` CORS preflight requests (HTTP 204).\\n  - Routes `POST` to increment counter.\\n  - Routes `GET` to read counter.\\n  - Returns HTTP 405 for unsupported HTTP methods.\\n  - Returns HTTP 400 for malformed input or invalid `pageId`.\\n  - Returns HTTP 500 for missing config or database errors.\\n\\n### 2. Testing & Tooling\\n\\n- [`package.json`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/package.json): Configured with `@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb`, `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`, `esbuild`, `jest`, `ts-jest`, and `aws-sdk-client-mock`.\\n- [`jest.config.js`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/jest.config.js): Unit test configuration with `ts-jest` and coverage thresholds.\\n- [`tests/counter.test.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/tests/counter.test.ts): Unit tests for DynamoDB atomic increments and reads.\\n- [`tests/handler.test.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/tests/handler.test.ts): End-to-end event routing, CORS, query/body parsing, and error handling.\\n- [`tests/config.test.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/tests/config.test.ts): Environment variable validation tests.\\n- [`tests/utils.test.ts`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/tests/utils.test.ts): Sanitization, validation, and CORS tests.\\n- [`README.md`](https://github.com/rensaputra/visitor-counter-api/blob/d180e02969a307162b1cab1ee1589d10396f2bd8/README.md): Documentation including DynamoDB table creation, IAM permissions, API Gateway HTTP API setup, and frontend client snippet.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Validation Results\\n\\n### 1. TypeScript Strict Typecheck\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm run typecheck\\n```\\n\\n> Passed with 0 errors.\\n\\n### 2. Jest Test Suite with Coverage\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm run test:coverage\\n```\\n\\n```\\nPASS tests/config.test.ts\\nPASS tests/utils.test.ts\\nPASS tests/counter.test.ts\\nPASS tests/handler.test.ts\\n\\n------------|---------|----------|---------|---------|-------------------\\nFile        | % Stmts | % Branch | % Funcs | % Lines | Uncovered Line #s\\n------------|---------|----------|---------|---------|-------------------\\nAll files   |    96.8 |    90.69 |   85.71 |    96.8 |\\n config.ts  |     100 |    88.88 |     100 |     100 |\\n counter.ts |     100 |       80 |     100 |     100 |\\n handler.ts |    93.1 |      100 |      50 |    93.1 |\\n utils.ts   |   97.29 |    94.11 |     100 |   97.29 |\\n------------|---------|----------|---------|---------|-------------------\\n\\nTest Suites: 4 passed, 4 total\\nTests:       33 passed, 33 total\\n```\\n\\n### 3. Build & Package Verification\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm run package\\n```\\n\\n- Bundled via `esbuild` to CommonJS Node.js 20 target.\\n- Produced `dist/visitor-counter.zip` (577 KB), verified `dist/index.js` exports `handler`."},{"id":"/2026/09/17/how-to-setup-1-husky-and-lint-staged","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2026/09/17/how-to-setup-1-husky-and-lint-staged","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-17-how-to-setup-1-husky-and-lint-staged.mdx","title":"How to setup Husky and Lint-Staged","description":"In modern software development, maintaining code quality and consistency across a team is crucial. When multiple developers work on a single codebase, differences in formatting, syntax styles, and simple errors can lead to messy pull requests and broken builds. This is where Git hooks, specifically pre-commit hooks, become incredibly valuable.","date":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"CI/CD","permalink":"/blog/tags/ci-cd","description":"Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment"}],"readingTime":3.87,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Rendy E. Saputra","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/rendy"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra/","github":"https://github.com/rensaputra","Website":"https://res.id.au"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/rensaputra.png","key":"rendy"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"How to setup Husky and Lint-Staged","authors":["rendy"],"tags":["ci/cd"],"date":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Walkthrough: Web Visitor Counter Lambda","permalink":"/blog/2026/09/18/lambda-web-visitor-counter"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to setup Eslint, Prettier and CodeQL","permalink":"/blog/how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql"}},"content":"In modern software development, maintaining code quality and consistency across a team is crucial. When multiple developers work on a single codebase, differences in formatting, syntax styles, and simple errors can lead to messy pull requests and broken builds. This is where Git hooks, specifically pre-commit hooks, become incredibly valuable.\\n\\nTwo of the most powerful tools in the JavaScript ecosystem for managing Git hooks and code quality are **Husky** and **Lint-Staged**.\\n\\nIn this post, we\'ll explore what these tools are, why they are important, how to set them up, and how to test your configuration locally.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What are Husky and Lint-Staged?\\n\\n### Husky\\n\\nHusky is a tool that allows you to easily tie scripts to Git hooks. Git hooks are scripts that Git executes before or after events such as: `commit`, `push`, and `receive`. Husky makes it simple to manage these hooks within your `package.json` or as separate script files, ensuring they are automatically installed for everyone who works on the project.\\n\\n### Lint-Staged\\n\\nLint-Staged is a tool that allows you to run linters (like ESLint, Prettier, or cspell) against staged git files and don\'t let it slip into your codebase. Running linters on the entire project can be slow. Lint-Staged solves this by only running checks on files that are actively being modified and staged for a commit.\\n\\n## Why is it Important?\\n\\n1. **Catches Errors Early**: By running linting, formatting, and spell checks on every commit, you catch simple mistakes before they even leave your local machine or trigger a CI build.\\n2. **Enforces Consistency**: It ensures that all code pushed to the repository adheres to the team\'s defined standards (e.g., Prettier formatting, ESLint rules).\\n3. **Improves Code Reviews**: When the code is consistently formatted, code reviews can focus on the actual logic and architecture rather than nitpicking spacing or syntax issues.\\n4. **Saves CI Time**: Failing fast locally means your continuous integration (CI) pipeline doesn\'t have to waste time running jobs that fail on trivial style errors.\\n\\n## How to Set It Up\\n\\nLet\'s walk through setting up Husky and Lint-Staged to run ESLint, Prettier, and a spell checker (`cspell`) on pre-commit.\\n\\n### 1. Install Dependencies\\n\\nFirst, ensure you have Husky, Lint-Staged, and your desired linters installed as `devDependencies`:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm install --save-dev husky lint-staged eslint prettier cspell\\n```\\n\\n### 2. Initialize Husky\\n\\nRun the Husky initialization script. This sets up the `.husky` directory and prepares Git to use it:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpx husky init\\n```\\n\\nThis command typically adds a `prepare` script in your `package.json` to ensure Husky is installed automatically after `npm install`.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"prepare\\": \\"husky\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 3. Create a Pre-commit Hook\\n\\nNext, we need to instruct Husky to run `lint-staged` before every commit.\\n\\nIf `npx husky init` created a default `pre-commit` hook (usually echoing something or running `npm test`), you can overwrite it. Run the following command or edit the `.husky/pre-commit` file directly:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \\"npx lint-staged\\" > .husky/pre-commit\\n```\\n\\n### 4. Configure Lint-Staged\\n\\nFinally, configure `lint-staged` in your `package.json` to define which tools run on which files. Here is an example configuration:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"lint-staged\\": {\\n    \\"**/*.{md,mdx}\\": \\"cspell lint\\",\\n    \\"**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,json,md,mdx,css}\\": \\"prettier --write\\",\\n    \\"src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}\\": \\"eslint\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Explanation of the config:**\\n\\n- `**/*.{md,mdx}`: Runs the spell checker (`cspell`) on all Markdown and MDX files.\\n- `**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,json,md,mdx,css}`: Runs Prettier to automatically format code. The `--write` flag tells Prettier to save the formatted changes back to the files.\\n- `src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}`: Runs ESLint on your source code to catch potential logic errors or rule violations.\\n\\n## How to Test It Locally\\n\\nOnce configured, testing the setup is straightforward.\\n\\n1. **Make a Change**: Modify a file that matches your lint-staged configuration. For example, add some unformatted code to a JavaScript file or a typo to a Markdown file.\\n2. **Stage the File**:\\n   ```bash\\n   git add path/to/your/file.js\\n   ```\\n3. **Attempt a Commit**:\\n   ```bash\\n   git commit -m \\"Test husky setup\\"\\n   ```\\n\\n**What should happen?**\\n\\n- Husky intercepts the commit process.\\n- It executes `npx lint-staged`.\\n- Lint-Staged looks at the staged file (`file.js`).\\n- It runs the configured tools (e.g., `prettier --write` and `eslint`).\\n- **If everything passes:** The formatted file is added to the commit, and the commit succeeds.\\n- **If a linter fails (e.g., ESLint finds an error):** The commit is aborted, and the error output is displayed in your terminal, forcing you to fix the issue before committing again.\\n\\nBy setting up Husky and Lint-Staged, you automate quality control directly within the developer workflow, leading to cleaner codebases and more efficient teams. Happy coding!"},{"id":"how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-17-how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql.mdx","title":"How to setup Eslint, Prettier and CodeQL","description":"In modern web development, maintaining a clean, secure, and consistent codebase is paramount, especially when working on teams. This is where tools like ESLint, Prettier, and CodeQL come in.","date":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"CI/CD","permalink":"/blog/tags/ci-cd","description":"Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment"}],"readingTime":3.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Rendy E. Saputra","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/rendy"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra/","github":"https://github.com/rensaputra","Website":"https://res.id.au"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/rensaputra.png","key":"rendy"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"How to setup Eslint, Prettier and CodeQL","authors":["rendy"],"tags":["ci/cd"],"slug":"how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to setup Husky and Lint-Staged","permalink":"/blog/2026/09/17/how-to-setup-1-husky-and-lint-staged"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome","permalink":"/blog/welcome"}},"content":"In modern web development, maintaining a clean, secure, and consistent codebase is paramount, especially when working on teams. This is where tools like ESLint, Prettier, and CodeQL come in.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n- **ESLint** is a powerful static code analysis tool that finds and fixes problems in your JavaScript or TypeScript code. It ensures that your code adheres to specific stylistic guidelines and catches potential runtime errors before the code is even executed.\\n- **Prettier** is an opinionated code formatter. Its sole job is to take your code and re-format it to ensure visual consistency across the entire project. It ends debates over spaces versus tabs, trailing commas, and line length.\\n- **CodeQL** is a semantic code analysis engine developed by GitHub. It allows you to query your code as if it were a database to find security vulnerabilities and bugs. It is highly popular because it integrates seamlessly into the GitHub ecosystem.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installing packages\\n\\nTo get started, we need to install the necessary packages for ESLint and Prettier. We don\'t need to install CodeQL locally as it is provided as a free service via GitHub Actions and runs directly in the CI/CD pipeline.\\n\\nRun the following command in your terminal to install the necessary development dependencies:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm install -D eslint prettier eslint-config-prettier eslint-plugin-prettier eslint-plugin-react eslint-plugin-react-hooks @typescript-eslint/parser @typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin\\n```\\n\\nNext, you will need to create the configuration files for these tools in the root directory of your project.\\n\\n### ESLint Configuration (`.eslintrc.js`)\\n\\nCreate a `.eslintrc.js` file and add the following configuration to integrate ESLint with React, TypeScript, and Prettier:\\n\\n```javascript\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  env: {\\n    browser: true,\\n    es2021: true,\\n    node: true,\\n  },\\n  extends: [\\n    \'eslint:recommended\',\\n    \'plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended\',\\n    \'plugin:react/recommended\',\\n    \'plugin:react-hooks/recommended\',\\n    \'plugin:prettier/recommended\',\\n  ],\\n  parser: \'@typescript-eslint/parser\',\\n  parserOptions: {\\n    ecmaFeatures: {\\n      jsx: true,\\n    },\\n    ecmaVersion: 12,\\n    sourceType: \'module\',\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\'react\', \'@typescript-eslint\', \'prettier\'],\\n  rules: {\\n    \'prettier/prettier\': \'warn\',\\n    \'react/react-in-jsx-scope\': \'off\',\\n    \'@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars\': [\'warn\', { argsIgnorePattern: \'^_\' }],\\n  },\\n  settings: {\\n    react: {\\n      version: \'detect\',\\n    },\\n  },\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### Prettier Configuration (`.prettierrc`)\\n\\nCreate a `.prettierrc` file to define your code formatting rules:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"semi\\": true,\\n  \\"trailingComma\\": \\"all\\",\\n  \\"singleQuote\\": true,\\n  \\"printWidth\\": 100,\\n  \\"tabWidth\\": 2\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Configure package.json\\n\\nTo easily execute ESLint and Prettier, we need to add several scripts to our `package.json` file.\\n\\nUpdate the `\\"scripts\\"` block in your `package.json` to include the following lines:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"format\\": \\"prettier --write .\\",\\n    \\"format:check\\": \\"prettier --check .\\",\\n    \\"lint\\": \\"eslint \\\\\\"src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}\\\\\\"\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n- `\\"format\\"`: Runs Prettier across the entire project directory and overwrites the files with the properly formatted code.\\n- `\\"format:check\\"`: Runs Prettier in check mode, outputting any files that are not formatted correctly without modifying them. This is particularly useful for CI environments.\\n- `\\"lint\\"`: Runs ESLint targeting all JavaScript and TypeScript files within the `src/` directory to catch errors and enforce code quality.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Ignore files\\n\\nWe don\'t want our linting and formatting tools to scan compiled output, third-party libraries, or cache folders. To prevent this, we must create two ignore files: `.eslintignore` and `.prettierignore`.\\n\\nCreate both `.eslintignore` and `.prettierignore` in the root of your project and add the following folders to them:\\n\\n```text\\nnode_modules\\nbuild\\ndist\\n.docusaurus\\ncoverage\\n```\\n\\n- **`node_modules`**: Contains third-party dependencies which we should not lint or format.\\n- **`build` & `dist`**: Compiled production outputs.\\n- **`.docusaurus`**: Docusaurus cache and auto-generated build files.\\n- **`coverage`**: Test coverage reporting output directory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\nNow the most important part: ensuring our setup works locally.\\n\\nTo test that Prettier is correctly formatting files, run the following command in your terminal:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm run format\\n```\\n\\n**Expected Output:** Prettier will scan through your project and automatically reformat any file that does not meet the standards defined in `.prettierrc`. The terminal will list the files it has processed.\\n\\nTo check for any potential code quality issues using ESLint, run:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpm run lint\\n```\\n\\n**Expected Output:** ESLint will scan the files in the `src/` directory. If your code is completely valid and follows all rules, the command will silently exit without any output. If there are code errors or stylistic violations, it will print a detailed report indicating the exact file, line number, and error/warning message.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## GitHub Actions CI Integration\\n\\nTo ensure no code regressions are introduced into the main branch, these tools are executed automatically in our CI pipeline.\\n\\nIn our `.github/workflows/ci.yml` file, we added steps to invoke both our formatting check (`npm run format:check`) and our linting script (`npm run lint`). If any of these scripts fail, the CI run will fail, blocking the pull request from being merged.\\n\\nAdditionally, a separate `.github/workflows/codeql-analysis.yml` workflow was introduced. This workflow initializes the CodeQL environment, builds the project (if necessary), and analyzes the codebase for common vulnerabilities, reporting them directly in the GitHub Security tab of the repository."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-17-welcome.mdx","title":"Welcome","description":"Welcome to my new blog! This space will be used to share my thoughts, experiences, and technical notes.","date":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/blog/tags/blog","description":"General blog posts"}],"readingTime":0.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Rendy E. Saputra","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/rendy"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rendyekasaputra/","github":"https://github.com/rensaputra","Website":"https://res.id.au"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/rensaputra.png","key":"rendy"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Welcome","authors":["rendy"],"tags":["blog"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to setup Eslint, Prettier and CodeQL","permalink":"/blog/how-to-setup-eslint-prettier-and-codeql"}},"content":"Welcome to my new blog! This space will be used to share my thoughts, experiences, and technical notes.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n### Docusaurus Blog Features Summary\\n\\nThis blog is powered by Docusaurus, which provides several great features out-of-the-box:\\n\\n- **MDX Support**: Blog posts support Docusaurus Markdown features, such as MDX. This allows the use of React components (like interactive buttons) and Admonitions (callouts/tips) directly inside the markdown files.\\n- **Truncation**: The `{/* truncate */}` comment can be used to limit the blog post size in the list view, keeping the main blog page clean and readable.\\n- **File Organization**: Posts can be standalone markdown files or placed within their own folders, which is convenient for co-locating images specific to a post.\\n- **Metadata**: The blog post date can be automatically extracted from filenames (e.g., `2026-09-17-welcome.mdx`), and author/tag metadata is easily managed via `authors.yml` and `tags.yml`."}]}}')}}]);